      "id": "R04-abstract",
      "text": "Short-horizon sampling-based MPC can miss later hazards and waste samples whose violation penalties drive their weights toward zero. Neural Shield VIMPC addresses these failures separately: a learned approximation of a policy's worst future constraint value brings post-horizon information into a discrete-time barrier descent constraint, while rollout resampling replaces unsafe states and control prefixes with safe counterparts when available. With 500 sampled trajectories, NS-MPPI alone among tested controllers had zero Drone crashes across evaluated horizons, including ten steps. At a 12 m/s AutoRally target speed, 15-step horizon and 30 trajectories per optimization iteration, its crash rate was 0.04 versus S-MPPI's 0.46, with CPU control at 73.64 Hz. These empirical improvements do not establish a safety guarantee: the learned barrier is unverified, and averaging constraint-satisfying controls can violate nonconvex safety constraints.",
