      "id": "R06-abstract",
      "text": "Visuomotor policies often generalize only near demonstrated object placements. DemoGen expands spatial coverage by transforming object-contact actions with their objects, replanning connecting free-space motions, and applying corresponding edits to segmented point clouds and recorded proprioceptive states. These paired observations and actions train DP3 without executing each synthetic trajectory; the method assumes reliable execution and uses inverse-kinematics control with absolute end-effector targets. Across eight real-world tasks, task-averaged success increased from 11.0% with source-only data to 74.6% with DemoGen. Each task used one human demonstration and two recorded replays. Generation targeted feasible evaluation placements, with small additional perturbations for single-arm tasks. Computation-only generation took 0.010 seconds per trajectory in one process, excluding collection and training. Single-view point-cloud transformations retain originally visible surfaces rather than reconstructing newly exposed views, and performance decreases farther from the demonstrated configuration.",
